{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>For the son of this maidservant shall not inherit.</b> But is it because he is sent away that it is impossible for him to return to his inheritance after his father's death? Rather, it appears that she is speaking about the inheritance of deeds, for Sarah saw the son of Hagar the Egyptian, whom she bore to Abraham, מְצַחֵק in sexual immorality — on account of his being the son of Hagar the Egyptian, who gave birth to one like herself, for all Egyptians are steeped in licentiousness, as it is stated (Ezekiel 23:20) ״וְזִרְמַת סוּסִים זִרְמָתָם״, and their offspring are like them. Therefore Sarah said to send him away so that Isaac would not learn from his deeds. And if you say: on the contrary, perhaps Isaac will turn him and restore him to the good — concerning this she said: I know through prophecy that he shall not inherit together with my son from the good deeds of his father Abraham, and therefore the concern remained that Isaac might learn from him. And more on the theme of this passage, see further below, parashat Chayei Sarah, at the verse ״וּשְׁמָהּ קְטוּרָה״ (Genesis 25:1)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:55.479638404Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}